# task-3 interleave mutation log — differential sensitivity proof

Scenario: swap ONE witness's r2-conversion operand in the 2-wide interleave
helper, in a SCRATCH build (product tree untouched). m4_mr_diff MUST diverge.

## Setup
- Scratch dir: /tmp/opencode/t3-scratch/mut
  - gpu_search_kernel.h  (copy of interleaved header + mutation)
  - gpu_search_kernel.cpp (copied TU — quoted #include resolves to including
    file's dir first, so -I tricks alone are insufficient; task-13 lesson)
- Mutation (line 230 of scratch header):
    BEFORE: uint64_t sqB = dev_MontMul(pB, r2, n, minv);
    AFTER:  uint64_t sqB = dev_MontMul(pB, minv, n, minv);
      /* MUTATION: witness-B r2 operand swapped to minv */
  (witness B of pair {2,3} gets its Montgomery encoding corrupted: converts
   pB into the M-domain with minv instead of r2)
- Mutated line verified present via grep BEFORE building.
- Per-arch objects rebuilt from the mutated TU mirroring CMake flags exactly:
    HIP_PLATFORM=amd    hipcc -O3 -Wall --offload-arch=gfx1201 ... -DSIEVE_KERNEL_ARCH=gfx1201
    HIP_PLATFORM=nvidia hipcc -O3 ... -DFF_BACKEND_NV=1 -DSIEVE_KERNEL_ARCH=sm_120 -x cu -arch=sm_120
  both rc=0.
- Scratch m4_mr_diff relinked from build/ with `ninja -t commands` output,
  substituting ONLY the two kernel objects + -o path (relative object paths
  require cwd=build/ — repo-root invocation fails ld, known gotcha).

## Result: DIVERGENCE as required (rc=1)
  device[0] AMD RX 9070 XT:  TOTAL tested=200391 divergences=2 -> MISMATCH
  device[1] RTX 5090:        TOTAL tested=200391 divergences=2 -> MISMATCH
  Catch location: bucket T:E odd sweep 5..119 @bound4
    DIVERGENCE n=13 host=PRIME device=composite
    DIVERGENCE n=19 host=PRIME device=composite
  Mechanism: corrupted chain-B encoding yields xB outside {oneM, nm1M}; the
  trailing-squaring loop never lands on oneM/nm1M within power2 steps, so the
  combined flag reports composite for these true primes. Larger-n buckets show
  0 divergences because chain A (witness 2) already decides nearly everything
  there — the OR makes a broken B invisible unless A passes; tiny primes are
  where B is decisive. Both cards identical => the differential oracle is
  sensitive to interleave restructuring on BOTH backends.

